long double is eight bytes in the tcc that micro-c builds.

WHY THIS IS HONEST RATHER THAN A WORKAROUND. micro-c maps float, double and
long double to ONE word-sized type. A tcc compiled by it therefore has an
eight-byte long double whatever its headers claim, and tcc's own guard notices:

    if (sizeof(long double) == LDOUBLE_SIZE)      8 == 16   no
    else if (sizeof(double) == LDOUBLE_SIZE)      8 == 16   no
    else if (0 == memcmp(ptr, &vtop->c.ld, LDOUBLE_SIZE))   reads 16 bytes
                                                            from an 8-byte
                                                            field
    else tcc_error("can't cross compile long double constants");   tccgen.c:7905

so mc-tcc could not emit a long double constant at all -- not even 0.0, because
the zero fast-path memcmps sixteen bytes out of eight. It stopped compiling
tcc's own source at tccpp.c:2398 and then at tccgen.c:2580.

Declaring LDOUBLE_SIZE 8 makes the compiler CONSISTENT WITH ITSELF: the second
branch above then matches and emits a double where a long double was asked for,
which is exactly what micro-c already does everywhere else. Without it the
compiler claims a 16-byte type it cannot produce.

WHAT IT COSTS, PLAINLY. Code generated by this tcc gives long double eight
bytes, where the aarch64 Linux ABI gives it sixteen. A program using long
double and linking against a normally-built libc would disagree about the size.
That is a real limitation and it is not a new one -- it is micro-c's float
unsoundness, which has been recorded since the first patch in this series,
becoming visible in the ABI rather than only in arithmetic. It applies to the
tcc micro-c builds, not to tcc generally.

TCC_USING_DOUBLE_FOR_LDOUBLE IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN. It is defined for
PE, macOS/arm64 and Win32 in tcc.h and selects double-based branches in the
float parser, but arm64-gen.c sets LDOUBLE_SIZE unconditionally, so the guard
above still fires. Both are wanted: the define picks the double code paths, and
this picks a size that code can satisfy.

MEASURED: with this and the header work, mc-tcc compiles the whole of tcc.c --
870,242 bytes of object -- where before it stopped in the float parser.
The twelve stay 12/12 and tests2 stays at 56 of 127.
--- a/arm64-gen.c
+++ b/arm64-gen.c
@@ -33,7 +33,7 @@
 
 #define PTR_SIZE 8
 
-#define LDOUBLE_SIZE 16
+#define LDOUBLE_SIZE 8
 #define LDOUBLE_ALIGN 16
 
 #define MAX_ALIGN 16
